SEC-3 (v2) — optional client-certificate verification, WANT mode
2026-08-10 · timelakedb-tls, TLS 1.3 on both listeners, client CA
configured via TIMELAKE_TLS_CLIENT_CA
================================================================================

THE CLAIM: the server requests a client certificate, verifies one if
presented, and serves the connection either way — so Grafana, Telegraf
and the bench harness are untouched while an agent that presents a
certificate is identified.

Startup, verbatim:
  SEC-3: client certificate verification enabled in WANT mode - a client
  that presents a certificate is identified, one that does not is still
  served   ca=/certs/ca.crt anchors=1

BOTH PATHS, HTTPS (run in-network; see the rig note below)
  anonymous, no client certificate      -> 200  {"status":"pass",...}
  with a verified client certificate    -> 200  {"status":"pass",...}
  anonymous line-protocol write         -> 204
  mTLS line-protocol write              -> 204
  query afterwards                      -> 2 rows (both writes landed)

BOTH PATHS, FLIGHT SQL
  anonymous (the Grafana shape)         -> connected, server answered
  with a client certificate             -> connected, server answered

  And the identity reached the server, which is the entire point:
    DEBUG timelake_flight: flight client authenticated
          peer=172.31.0.1:40132 identity=tributary-node-1

METRICS
  timelake_tls_client_ca_anchors 1
  timelake_tls_client_ca_last_reload_ok 1
  timelake_tls_client_auth_mode 1

  The anchor count is what makes a dual-CA roll observable: during an
  overlap it reads 2, and an operator can see the old anchor leave.

AT-7 REGRESSION: 19/19, unchanged with client auth enabled
  Certificate rotation under sustained load still passes every check —
  both listeners rotate in lockstep, an in-flight 19-second Flight SQL
  query completes correctly across the swap with an exact result, zero
  write errors, Telegraf over TLS logs no output errors, a corrupt
  renewal is rejected with the named alarm while the last-good pair
  keeps serving, and a good renewal restores health.

  The drill needed fixing, not the server. Its first run scored 18/19
  with "admin reload rejects corrupt pair" failing on
  HTTP 401 unauthenticated — because SEC-4 put /admin/tls/reload behind
  a session and the drill still called it anonymously. The behaviour
  itself was fine (the file-watcher path proved the alarm, the last-good
  serving and the recovery). The drill now logs in, rotates the seeded
  admin/admin credential, and uses the session, so it exercises the real
  authenticated path instead of routing around it.

WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT
  Want mode is NOT by itself a security control. An attacker declines to
  present a certificate and takes the anonymous path. Its value is
  entirely in what the two paths may then do differently:

    QuerySession::resolve()
      no certificate      claims trusted as asserted   (UNCHANGED)
      verified identity   claims INTERSECTED with grants

  So authenticating can only ever narrow what a caller sees, never widen
  it, and the anonymous path is exactly as it was. That makes the fix for
  SECURITY.md exposure 7 additive rather than a flag day. Four tests pin
  the rule, including that an identity with no grant policy recorded
  keeps its claims — `None` must mean "no policy" and not "deny all", or
  presenting a certificate would break a working client, which is the
  opposite of an additive migration.

RIG NOTE
  Windows curl links schannel, which refuses a private CA with
  "revocation status is unknown" (exit 60) and will not load a client
  certificate from PEM. Every check above therefore runs from a Linux
  container on the compose network — the same in-network discipline the
  performance work uses, for a different reason.

WORKSPACE
  95 tests, 0 failures, clippy clean, fmt clean.
  +5 client_auth tests: bundle loading and anchor counting, dual-CA
  overlap expressible, a bad bundle keeping last-good anchors with the
  alarm, an empty bundle refused rather than trusting nothing, and
  anonymous peers having no identity.
  +4 session tests for the intersection rule above.

NOT DONE YET
  HTTP /api/sql does not yet attach the identity: axum-server owns that
  accept loop, so it needs a custom Accept implementation to carry peer
  certificates into request extensions. Flight SQL — the path Grafana
  and the read side use — is wired. Named here rather than left to look
  finished.
